  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/5/5f/Fantastic_Four_Annual_Vol_1_1.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "The story opens with Namor at his coronation (in Atlantis), having finally found his long-lost undersea kingdom, and he vows retribution on the \"insolent human race\" for its crimes.&nbsp; Lady Dorma and Warlord Krang are present, the former happy to see her \"loved one\" again, while Krang reminds her she is pledged to him!&nbsp; Dorma is having none of it - Namor is her \"true love.\"&nbsp; Krang inwardly seethes at Namor's return.\n\nBack at FF HQ, we get the standard three pages of Ben and Johnny fighting each other (interrupting Ben's scathing response letter to the Yancy Street Gang).&nbsp; Reed of course shows up to put a stop to it, rolling Ben up, who hollers \"You can't do this to the idol of millions!\"&nbsp; Sue's only concern is that her \"expensive Dior and Saks Fifth Avenue dresses\" are ruined - \"Men!&nbsp; You're <i>all</i> beasts!\"\n\nReed says they're all on edge and need a vacation.&nbsp; He says he heard one experimental atomic sub encountered a sea monster in the middle of the Atlantic.&nbsp; He suggests a cruise to that general vicinity as a way of combining business with pleasure.&nbsp; Alicia arrives just then and insists on coming along.\n\nIt's not long before Johnny spots a sea monster, and the four investigate.&nbsp; Different fish are used to surround them in air bubbles and dragged down into the depths (<i>convenient</i>).&nbsp; There they are greeted by Namor, who informs them that his \"tele-fish\" spied on them, actually transmitting \"visual impressions\" from his spines (<i>again, convenient</i>).\n\nTurns out Namor was behind the appearances of the sea monster (<i>big surprise</i>).&nbsp; He wanted the FF to find him so he could deliver an ultimatum to the United Nations that from now on, the seas belong to him.&nbsp; All ships that venture into it will be turned back toward land.&nbsp; No aircraft either!&nbsp; He then sends the FF back to deliver his message, and Reed is impressed with the advanced technology that is used to return them home (on page 13 - see my additional note in the comments section below).\n\nReed soon addresses the UN, and we see one Communist member stomping his shoe on the table voting \"Nyet! Nyet! Nyet!\"&nbsp; But Reed anticipated resistance to the very grave threat that Namor and his undersea army represents, so a Dr. G. W. Falton (an authority on undersea life) addresses them to explain Namor's origin.&nbsp; He holds up a skull, saying it is \"Homo Mermanus, or Man of the Sea!\"&nbsp; He explains the parallel evolution of the undersea civilization, which became the legend of Atlantis, which largely was left alone.&nbsp; But in 1920, man made his first recorded contact with Homo Mermanus aboard the ice-breaker \"Oracle\" commanded by Captain Leonard McKenzie near the South Pole.&nbsp; Their destruction of a large iceberg rocked an undersea settlement, and the king sent his daughter, Princess Fen to investigate - they can survive in the air for about 5 hours at a time.&nbsp; She is discovered, and over the course of time, McKenzie and Fen fall in love and are married.&nbsp; The King, wondering about his daughter, dispatches a war party to engage the ship, and McKenzie is killed, but Fen had already become pregnant with what turned out to be Namor, able to breathe both air <i>and</i> water.&nbsp; He sums up his speech by saying \"This was Prince Namor, the Sub-Mariner, possibly the first mutant of our time!\"&nbsp; -  and the only true amphibian on Earth.\n\nReed then catches them up, pointing out that his undersea home was \"accidentally destroyed by an H-bomb test at sea!\"&nbsp; And he adds that the surface world must be resolute and \"fight him until he can threaten us no more!\"&nbsp; And then we learn that Dr. G. W. Falton is actually the Sub-Mariner in disguise, who upon hearing Reed's comments declares war upon the human race!\n\nSuddenly, NYC is under attack - Atlantis is invading!&nbsp; And by afternoon, Namor's forces take over the city.&nbsp; They try to encase FF HQ in cement, but the Torch counter-attacks, but they find that \"Namor's legions are attacking <i>everywhere</i>!\"&nbsp; Ben goes out and rounds up some of Namor's forces for Reed to study, and Reed notices the water-filled helmets.&nbsp; It's only a matter of coming up with a way to evaporate that water from their helmets.\"\n\nNamor stands in Central Park and gives out his edicts as conqueror, even going so far as to point out that they need to make sure \"humans never again gain control of Earth's surface!\"&nbsp; And the military is afraid to attack since there are 9 million people in the city they don't want to accidentally harm.&nbsp; But Reed is busy finishing his device.&nbsp; Sure enough, the helmet water begins to evaporate, driving the army back into the ocean.&nbsp; (<i>Strange that they must flee to the sea immediately - see notes below in the commentary section.</i>)\n\nThe invasion is thus halted by Mr. Fantastic, and Namor is livid and strikes back, attacking the FF, but Namor grabs Sue and flies away.&nbsp; Retreating to the oceans now with his prisoner, Namor vows (with Lady Dorma at his side) that he has only <i>begun</i> to fight.&nbsp; He attacks Johnny, hovering above their location, and Reed and Ben soon join him.&nbsp; A couple of pages of all-out action ensue, while Sue below pleads for both Namor and the FF to stop.&nbsp; Krang and Lady Dorma then realize that Namor actually loves Sue, and Dorma will have none of <i>that</i>!&nbsp; She attempts to destroy Sue, but Krang points out that if Namor loves the human, Dorma is free to be his!&nbsp; She smashes one of the portholes so let the water rush in and drown Sue, who attempts to kick herself back to the surface.&nbsp; But she gets tangled and is only noticed in time by the battling Ben and Namor - she needs hospitalization immediately!&nbsp; Namor returns to the undersea craft where Sue just escaped from and orders everyone out - Dorma is incensed!&nbsp; Namor helping the enemy?\n\nHe returns to the surface to take Sue to the hospital.&nbsp; Reed knows it's no trick, \"for Namor, in his own strange way, loves Sue as much as we do!\"&nbsp; They get back to Manhattan a couple of hours later, having to take the time to repair the damage Namor caused to their own craft.&nbsp; But they find Sue in the hospital, recovering nicely.\n\nThey find Namor walking through an angry crowd, who jeer at his failure to take over the surface world.&nbsp; Namor shoves them aside and returns to his craft, only to go home and find everything deserted!&nbsp; The once-adoring kingdom has sensed that Namor has betrayed them by giving his heart to a surface woman.&nbsp; Namor is left all alone - \"a king without a kingdom...Is there <i>never</i> to be a place for me - on the surface or in the sea?\"&nbsp; More is promised in future issues of the Fantastic Four!",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "[SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=bold; text=FOR THE RECORD:]\n[BR] This is a review of the first of the three stories found in this magazine.\n[BR] My review on the second story is posted elsewhere.\n\n[DIVIDER]\n\nWhile by no means was this a classic tale, it was nonetheless a solid, not to mention <i>lengthy</i> one (as indicated too on the splash page) that was well-paced overall, though there were a few moments that they seemed to rush over.&nbsp; One wonders if they might have done better to eliminate the standard 2-3 pages of Ben vs. Johnny at the beginning and instead replaced it with a more spaced-out sequence later when Namor takes over the city, which seemed to happen in the blink of an eye.\n\nOne also wonders why it is that the people of Atlantis were wearing water-filled helmets at all when it was explained earlier in the story that Princess Fen could survive up to five hours outside of the sea.&nbsp; Was she just special that way and this is not a normal ability that all the people of Atlantis share?&nbsp; Perhaps so, because Namor becomes a mutant.&nbsp; Did he inherit some mutant ability from his mother?&nbsp; Pages 32 and 33 show that Krang and Dorma have no problem surviving for a time in an air-filled compartment, so why did the members of the invasion force have to return to the water <i>immediately</i>?&nbsp; (Also, these pages show how pathetic Sue's offensive ability is as part of the FF - all you have to do is tie her hands behind her back, and she's essentially helpless.)\n\nI <i>loved</i> the second-to-last panel of page 13, where Ben says \"What an <i>egg-head</i>!&nbsp; Reed, if they ever strapped ya in the electric chair, you'd probably be explainin' how the blamed thing <i>works</i> while they pulled the switch!\"&nbsp; (<i>Heh</i>, well-done, Stan!)\n\nOnce again, the crazy fish and machines are too in-your-face for this to be more than a decent story.&nbsp; The people of Atlantis going so far as to <i>abandon</i> their own homes instead of simply staying and kicking Namor out is a bit far-fetched as well.&nbsp; And somehow, I suspect any reader worth his salt suspected the story might come down to Namor's love of Sue - ultimately leading to his downfall.\n\nHowever, the origin of the Sub-Mariner and the introduction of Dorma and Krang were worthwhile, though also a bit rushed, and quite honestly, <i>neither</i> of them seem very likeable.&nbsp; It seems clear we haven't heard the last from them - as I said, a solid tale that leaves the reader wanting a bit more - and what more can you ask for?&nbsp; These aspects are worth elevating this book slightly above mid-grade - a good, but not <i>great</i> read!\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\n\nThere are a few pin-up pages of the FF's gallery of villains, along with a two-page spread featuring \"Questions and Answers About the Fantastic Four\" where they address some of the fans' various questions, which was another nice touch, very much in the same vein as what they did in [LINK:1962:FF_11_ff1|FF #11].\n\n\nThe second story in the annual is a re-telling of Spider-Man's first encounter with the FF, but <i>with additional detail</i>, apparently in response to \"countless requests\" to re-tell it, but devote more space.&nbsp; \"And so - because you requested it...\"&nbsp; The story is written by Stan Lee, drawn by Jack Kirby and inked by Steve Ditko, so all the players are involved here.&nbsp; (Though Kirby just doesn't seem to be able to get Spider-Man's mask right.&nbsp; Note the cover where it looks like a checkerboard pattern, as opposed to the way it looks on the Ditko-inked pages within.)\n\nIt's largely a true re-telling, with only additional battle panels, where Spider-Man takes on each FF member individually.&nbsp; Thus, I feel no need to give it its own rating, though I don't really see how it improves things much at all, except to give the reader a better sense of where Spider-Man ranks as a hero in his own right - very impressive encounters early in the young hero's career, but largely brings nothing new to the table.\n\nThe annual concludes with a reprinted version of the first half of [LINK:1961:FF_1|FF #1], recounting their origin.",
  "letters_page": "Note that there's a <i>great</i> letter about this annual published in FF #23 by Stephen Foston (the last letter published) - <i>really</i> great letter about how excited he was to pick up the annual he couldn't find anywhere!&nbsp; I loved reading it!",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}